00:01:33.550 --> 00:01:35.560
So our first question
of the day comes

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:40.990
from Krishnadev M., who asks,
does two-factor authentication

00:01:40.990 --> 00:01:43.090
fully protect us?

00:01:43.090 --> 00:01:45.560
So Seth, we'll start
with you for this one.

00:01:45.560 --> 00:01:48.047
I know you have a lot of
background in this area.

00:01:48.047 --> 00:01:48.880
SETH VARGO: Awesome.

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:51.380
Thanks, Stephanie, and thanks,
Krishnadev, for the question.

00:01:51.380 --> 00:01:55.030
So what you have to think about
when you think about MFA, which

00:01:55.030 --> 00:01:57.280
is multifactor
authentication, or 2FA,

00:01:57.280 --> 00:01:58.990
which is two-factor
authentication,

00:01:58.990 --> 00:02:01.240
is defense in depth.

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:04.660
We know that a username is
kind of public information.

00:02:04.660 --> 00:02:07.060
A password is something private.

00:02:07.060 --> 00:02:09.729
And then multifactor
auth or two-factor auth

00:02:09.729 --> 00:02:11.590
is something else
that an attacker

00:02:11.590 --> 00:02:12.970
would need to
compromise in order

00:02:12.970 --> 00:02:15.070
to gain access to your account.

00:02:15.070 --> 00:02:18.970
If you ever see a question
like, is something 100% secure,

00:02:18.970 --> 00:02:22.480
or does this fully protect
us, the answer is always no.

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:24.460
Nothing is 100% secure.

00:02:24.460 --> 00:02:26.500
Nothing is 100% preventative.

00:02:26.500 --> 00:02:28.870
But it's all about
defense in depth.

00:02:28.870 --> 00:02:32.440
We like to talk about
layers of an onion

00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:33.640
when we talk about security.

00:02:33.640 --> 00:02:35.950
But I actually like
to talk about security

00:02:35.950 --> 00:02:37.330
in terms of medieval times.

00:02:37.330 --> 00:02:39.310
See, the thing is,
we didn't really

00:02:39.310 --> 00:02:43.450
invent defense in depth in
the computer security space.

00:02:43.450 --> 00:02:46.690
If you think about a
castle, most people

00:02:46.690 --> 00:02:48.292
are familiar with a moat, right?

00:02:48.292 --> 00:02:50.500
You surround the castle with
a moat and a drawbridge,

00:02:50.500 --> 00:02:52.300
and you put scary
alligators in the moat.

00:02:52.300 --> 00:02:55.720
And then people have to cross
that moat to get to the castle.

00:02:55.720 --> 00:02:58.780
But if that's all an
attacker had to do,

00:02:58.780 --> 00:03:00.940
they could just swim
really fast and go

00:03:00.940 --> 00:03:03.893
get the prince, princess,
king, queen, whatever.

00:03:03.893 --> 00:03:06.310
Actually, if we look at the
way that castles were designed

00:03:06.310 --> 00:03:09.130
and architected
during medieval times,

00:03:09.130 --> 00:03:10.690
they practiced defense in depth.

00:03:10.690 --> 00:03:13.780
It wasn't just a moat, but
the actual architecture

00:03:13.780 --> 00:03:16.870
of the castle was designed
such that the prince, princess,

00:03:16.870 --> 00:03:19.450
king, queen were
maximally protected.

00:03:19.450 --> 00:03:23.613
Just as an example, the
stairways in medieval castles

00:03:23.613 --> 00:03:25.030
were designed such
that they would

00:03:25.030 --> 00:03:26.620
curve up and to the right.

00:03:26.620 --> 00:03:30.130
And this was primarily
so that the defender,

00:03:30.130 --> 00:03:32.290
the people defending the
castle, had an advantage.

00:03:32.290 --> 00:03:34.748
They had a height advantage
since they would be coming down

00:03:34.748 --> 00:03:36.790
the stairs, and
since most people

00:03:36.790 --> 00:03:39.050
were predominantly
right-handed during that era,

00:03:39.050 --> 00:03:40.210
they had a swing advantage.

00:03:40.210 --> 00:03:42.970
They were swinging at a downward
angle, gravity giving them

00:03:42.970 --> 00:03:45.220
favor, and with
their dominant hand.

00:03:45.220 --> 00:03:47.080
When you compare this
to the attacker, who

00:03:47.080 --> 00:03:51.560
is swinging at a back swing,
up a hill, on an incline,

00:03:51.560 --> 00:03:52.970
they have a disadvantage.

00:03:52.970 --> 00:03:56.270
So this idea of defense in depth
is really not a new concept.

00:03:56.270 --> 00:03:58.660
It's existed for hundreds
of thousands of years.

00:03:58.660 --> 00:03:59.980
We're just applying it.

00:03:59.980 --> 00:04:02.590
So nothing is 100% secure.

00:04:02.590 --> 00:04:04.660
But that doesn't mean you
still shouldn't do it.

00:04:04.660 --> 00:04:07.150
You should definitely adopt
multifactor authentication

00:04:07.150 --> 00:04:09.970
or two-factor authentication
if your upstream account

00:04:09.970 --> 00:04:11.135
provider supports it.

00:04:11.135 --> 00:04:12.010
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah.

00:04:12.010 --> 00:04:14.650
I'm surprised we don't
have curved stairways

00:04:14.650 --> 00:04:16.839
in our data centers, at
least not that I've seen.

00:04:16.839 --> 00:04:18.610
But maybe we will.

00:04:18.610 --> 00:04:21.190
But it's cool how we
translate those concepts

00:04:21.190 --> 00:04:22.870
into our logical security, too.

00:04:22.870 --> 00:04:26.290
And one way that I know our
security specialists here

00:04:26.290 --> 00:04:29.950
think of it is to explain
2FA as something you know

00:04:29.950 --> 00:04:31.420
and something you have, right?

00:04:31.420 --> 00:04:33.970
So something you know, like
the password or the PIN number,

00:04:33.970 --> 00:04:36.250
and then something you have,
like your phone, token,

00:04:36.250 --> 00:04:37.910
or other digital device.

00:04:37.910 --> 00:04:39.700
And then the other
thing is something

00:04:39.700 --> 00:04:42.520
you are, so something unique
to your physical being,

00:04:42.520 --> 00:04:45.370
like biometrics, or
fingerprints, palm prints,

00:04:45.370 --> 00:04:47.650
retina scans, GPS location.

00:04:47.650 --> 00:04:50.410
I know that I went through that
at the data center, which we'll

00:04:50.410 --> 00:04:51.800
talk about in a little bit.

00:04:51.800 --> 00:04:54.310
But yeah, it's all an
extra layer of protection,

00:04:54.310 --> 00:04:55.700
like you said.

00:04:55.700 --> 00:04:56.200
All right.

00:04:56.200 --> 00:05:01.070
So moving on to our
second question--

00:05:01.070 --> 00:05:05.180
so we have a question from
Alessandro C., who asks,

00:05:05.180 --> 00:05:09.070
how can we secure a Google
Cloud Storage bucket, in which

00:05:09.070 --> 00:05:11.800
we store sensitive client CSVs?

00:05:11.800 --> 00:05:14.480
Are there any best
practices to consider?

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:16.972
So Seth, can you go ahead and
answer this one for us, too?

00:05:16.972 --> 00:05:17.680
SETH VARGO: Yeah.

00:05:17.680 --> 00:05:19.690
This is also a
great question that

00:05:19.690 --> 00:05:22.655
really leads into a discussion
around threat modeling.

00:05:22.655 --> 00:05:24.280
What are you trying
to protect against?

00:05:24.280 --> 00:05:25.905
What are the attack
vectors that you're

00:05:25.905 --> 00:05:27.010
trying to protect against?

00:05:27.010 --> 00:05:30.730
In case of Google Cloud
Storage, you can enforce ACLs,

00:05:30.730 --> 00:05:32.650
or "ack-els," access
control lists,

00:05:32.650 --> 00:05:35.300
on individual objects within
a Cloud Storage bucket.

00:05:35.300 --> 00:05:38.140
And you can govern those with
identity and access management,

00:05:38.140 --> 00:05:39.220
or IAM.

00:05:39.220 --> 00:05:41.290
So you can ensure
that only customer

00:05:41.290 --> 00:05:44.620
A has access to object
A and customer B only

00:05:44.620 --> 00:05:47.470
has access to CSB B.

00:05:47.470 --> 00:05:50.680
Now, you could also
add additional controls

00:05:50.680 --> 00:05:53.540
on top of that, on top of
identity and access management,

00:05:53.540 --> 00:05:58.030
such as VPC Service Controls,
where you can define a service

00:05:58.030 --> 00:06:01.630
perimeter around a Cloud Storage
bucket or other Google Cloud

00:06:01.630 --> 00:06:04.630
resources so that they're
not accessible outside

00:06:04.630 --> 00:06:07.300
of authorized networks or
outside of other projects

00:06:07.300 --> 00:06:08.590
that you've pre-configured.

00:06:08.590 --> 00:06:11.920
So really, there is
no checklist box,

00:06:11.920 --> 00:06:14.200
and your Cloud Storage bucket
is now magically secure.

00:06:14.200 --> 00:06:16.450
You need to think about your
threat model, what you're

00:06:16.450 --> 00:06:19.330
protecting against, what
are the access patterns,

00:06:19.330 --> 00:06:22.390
and how can you most restrict
those resources to only

00:06:22.390 --> 00:06:24.130
be accessed by
the customers when

00:06:24.130 --> 00:06:27.545
they need to access it from
where they need to access it.

00:06:27.545 --> 00:06:28.420
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah.

00:06:28.420 --> 00:06:30.820
I think VPC Service
Controls are great.

00:06:30.820 --> 00:06:33.820
As you said, you define
fine-grained perimeter controls

00:06:33.820 --> 00:06:36.460
that enforce your security
posture across your Google

00:06:36.460 --> 00:06:37.240
Cloud Services.

00:06:37.240 --> 00:06:40.090
I've used it with BigQuery
and Cloud Storage.

00:06:40.090 --> 00:06:43.450
And it helps, really,
with data exfiltration.

00:06:43.450 --> 00:06:45.430
And then I think you
might have talked

00:06:45.430 --> 00:06:48.490
a little bit about
context-aware access as well.

00:06:48.490 --> 00:06:52.918
Is that similar to
identity-aware proxy?

00:06:52.918 --> 00:06:54.710
SETH VARGO: So it's a
little bit different.

00:06:54.710 --> 00:06:59.400
So context-aware access is like,
given my current context, which

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:02.130
could be like my device,
my mobile phone, my laptop,

00:07:02.130 --> 00:07:04.860
or perhaps my
location, my network,

00:07:04.860 --> 00:07:06.880
do I have permission to
access this resource?

00:07:06.880 --> 00:07:11.760
So even though I am Seth,
can Seth access this resource

00:07:11.760 --> 00:07:13.470
in this context, right?

00:07:13.470 --> 00:07:16.440
Maybe I can only access it when
I'm at home on my home network,

00:07:16.440 --> 00:07:18.510
but I can't access
it if I'm at a hotel

00:07:18.510 --> 00:07:20.220
on an untrusted network.

00:07:20.220 --> 00:07:23.160
Identity-aware proxy is
a Google Cloud product

00:07:23.160 --> 00:07:26.280
that you can put in front
of one of your applications

00:07:26.280 --> 00:07:30.660
and resources that let you
enforce these types of policies

00:07:30.660 --> 00:07:32.590
so that you don't have
to modify your code.

00:07:32.590 --> 00:07:34.200
So it sits in
front of your code.

00:07:34.200 --> 00:07:36.300
So you can apply
these policies so you

00:07:36.300 --> 00:07:38.892
don't have to modify your
own code in order to enforce

00:07:38.892 --> 00:07:39.850
some of these policies.

00:07:39.850 --> 00:07:42.028
So they're related,
but slightly different.

00:07:42.028 --> 00:07:43.320
STEPHANIE WONG: Got it, got it.

00:07:43.320 --> 00:07:45.810
So identity-aware proxy in
front of your sensitive app,

00:07:45.810 --> 00:07:48.750
it's like if you want to
grant access to all employees

00:07:48.750 --> 00:07:51.720
if they're from a trusted
corporate device versus

00:07:51.720 --> 00:07:55.380
granting access to employees in
like a privileged access group

00:07:55.380 --> 00:08:00.000
if the URL path starts
with something like /admin.

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:02.760
OK, so moving on to
question number three--

00:08:02.760 --> 00:08:07.240
the next question is
from Dilipxs, who asks,

00:08:07.240 --> 00:08:09.330
how do you check if
there are any security

00:08:09.330 --> 00:08:11.170
breaches in the Cloud?

00:08:11.170 --> 00:08:13.950
Carter, let's go to
you for this one.

00:08:13.950 --> 00:08:17.700
CARTER MORGAN: Thank you,
Stephanie and Dilipxs.

00:08:17.700 --> 00:08:20.560
Really, this comes down to
two things, in my opinion,

00:08:20.560 --> 00:08:22.210
one which I'm
assuming is kind of

00:08:22.210 --> 00:08:24.210
taken care of-- but maybe
you shouldn't assume--

00:08:24.210 --> 00:08:26.408
but having policies
in place to say

00:08:26.408 --> 00:08:28.200
who should be able to
access what and when.

00:08:28.200 --> 00:08:30.390
And Seth was kind of
touching on that before when

00:08:30.390 --> 00:08:31.890
he said there's
context, and there's

00:08:31.890 --> 00:08:34.590
things like IAM to
say which roles can

00:08:34.590 --> 00:08:36.220
access this safely and fine.

00:08:36.220 --> 00:08:38.520
So if you've set that up,
that's the first step.

00:08:38.520 --> 00:08:41.190
Because then you can do
things like have logging

00:08:41.190 --> 00:08:44.610
set up to say, what people
accessed accounts when,

00:08:44.610 --> 00:08:46.270
at what time?

00:08:46.270 --> 00:08:48.030
And then you can start
to make decisions

00:08:48.030 --> 00:08:49.510
based on these activities.

00:08:49.510 --> 00:08:51.630
So if people are
accessing information

00:08:51.630 --> 00:08:53.910
at a really, really late
point in time, you might say,

00:08:53.910 --> 00:08:54.700
that's suspicious.

00:08:54.700 --> 00:08:55.800
Let's look into this more.

00:08:55.800 --> 00:08:58.800
But that again starts with
having some policies set up

00:08:58.800 --> 00:09:03.185
in place to say who should
be able to access what, when.

00:09:03.185 --> 00:09:05.560
Yeah, and then I think another
thing that's really useful

00:09:05.560 --> 00:09:08.140
is having, basically, a
hub where you can find

00:09:08.140 --> 00:09:09.432
this information pretty easily.

00:09:09.432 --> 00:09:11.265
And so these are things
Google Cloud kind of

00:09:11.265 --> 00:09:12.160
provides that I like.

00:09:12.160 --> 00:09:15.850
But I think those are the
first steps to finding out

00:09:15.850 --> 00:09:17.045
about security breaches.

00:09:17.045 --> 00:09:17.920
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah.

00:09:17.920 --> 00:09:20.770
And I think one of those hubs,
for example, is our Security

00:09:20.770 --> 00:09:21.940
Command Center, right?

00:09:21.940 --> 00:09:24.850
And I have seen it evolve
over the past few years,

00:09:24.850 --> 00:09:27.520
and they've done a really great
job at designing dashboards

00:09:27.520 --> 00:09:30.500
like Security Health Analytics,
where you can see at a glance

00:09:30.500 --> 00:09:33.880
the number of projects you have,
what resources are deployed,

00:09:33.880 --> 00:09:35.800
and then manage which
service accounts have

00:09:35.800 --> 00:09:37.090
been added or removed.

00:09:37.090 --> 00:09:39.790
And then it actually even gives
you actionable recommendations

00:09:39.790 --> 00:09:43.420
when it surfaces any
misconfigurations or compliance

00:09:43.420 --> 00:09:47.320
violations, and has some
more advanced features, too,

00:09:47.320 --> 00:09:51.160
like detecting threats for
common container attacks.

00:09:51.160 --> 00:09:52.570
And we actually have a series.

00:09:52.570 --> 00:09:54.862
So I'm going to plug that
here, because I worked on it.

00:09:54.862 --> 00:09:58.150
But it's called "Getting Started
with Security Command Center."

00:09:58.150 --> 00:10:00.670
So go ahead and check that out.

00:10:00.670 --> 00:10:02.600
It's in our YouTube channel.

00:10:02.600 --> 00:10:03.710
So thank you, Carter.

00:10:03.710 --> 00:10:06.100
We're going to move on
to our next question.

00:10:06.100 --> 00:10:09.040
And it's from
Dheeraj P., who asks,

00:10:09.040 --> 00:10:14.080
are service accounts in some
way related to access scopes?

00:10:14.080 --> 00:10:15.913
So Carter, do you have
any thoughts on this?

00:10:15.913 --> 00:10:17.413
CARTER MORGAN: I
have some thoughts.

00:10:17.413 --> 00:10:19.870
I actually want to know more
about what access scopes are,

00:10:19.870 --> 00:10:22.287
because off the top of my head,
I don't know what that is.

00:10:22.287 --> 00:10:23.860
I might have to defer.

00:10:23.860 --> 00:10:26.290
But when I think about
service accounts,

00:10:26.290 --> 00:10:29.470
the first thing I think
about is automated access.

00:10:29.470 --> 00:10:32.090
So instead of having a person
manually log in to everything,

00:10:32.090 --> 00:10:34.540
what if I have an application
or a script that can log in

00:10:34.540 --> 00:10:37.600
and run things, update
something for me?

00:10:37.600 --> 00:10:40.030
And then when I think about
that, that automation,

00:10:40.030 --> 00:10:41.860
it's like, well,
how do I do this

00:10:41.860 --> 00:10:43.660
in a way that's safe or secure?

00:10:43.660 --> 00:10:47.022
I don't want to have to
take my personal password

00:10:47.022 --> 00:10:49.480
and just write it down somewhere
that a script can look at,

00:10:49.480 --> 00:10:51.050
because then it's very unsafe.

00:10:51.050 --> 00:10:53.630
And so service
accounts solve that.

00:10:53.630 --> 00:10:55.600
But when it comes into
the access scopes,

00:10:55.600 --> 00:11:01.388
I'm actually like, if Stephanie
or Seth have more context

00:11:01.388 --> 00:11:02.680
there, I would love to hear it.

00:11:02.680 --> 00:11:04.210
Because I want to
know about that, too.

00:11:04.210 --> 00:11:05.930
SETH VARGO: Yeah, I can
definitely chime in.

00:11:05.930 --> 00:11:07.870
So you have to kind of
forget about Google Cloud

00:11:07.870 --> 00:11:09.703
for a second, because
access scopes actually

00:11:09.703 --> 00:11:12.333
come from OAuth, which
is the authentication

00:11:12.333 --> 00:11:14.500
mechanism by which you
authenticate to Google Cloud.

00:11:14.500 --> 00:11:16.662
But it's an open
standard, and it's also

00:11:16.662 --> 00:11:18.370
how you kind of like
sign in with GitHub,

00:11:18.370 --> 00:11:19.840
sign in with Twitter, et cetera.

00:11:19.840 --> 00:11:23.140
And OAuth, the standard,
defines access scopes

00:11:23.140 --> 00:11:24.910
as the access control.

00:11:24.910 --> 00:11:27.880
So for example, let's say
you're giving someone access

00:11:27.880 --> 00:11:29.260
to your Twitter account.

00:11:29.260 --> 00:11:32.110
When you do that, you're
authorizing actions

00:11:32.110 --> 00:11:33.920
that they can take
on your behalf.

00:11:33.920 --> 00:11:36.400
So you may grant someone
read-only access.

00:11:36.400 --> 00:11:39.400
They can only read your tweets
and your direct messages.

00:11:39.400 --> 00:11:41.200
But you could also
grant them the ability

00:11:41.200 --> 00:11:42.550
to tweet on your behalf.

00:11:42.550 --> 00:11:44.320
You might do this
when you sign in

00:11:44.320 --> 00:11:47.740
with a tweet bot or some
third-party application.

00:11:47.740 --> 00:11:50.380
Those permissions
actually map to what

00:11:50.380 --> 00:11:54.010
we call OAuth scopes, which we
call access scopes on Google

00:11:54.010 --> 00:11:55.000
Cloud.

00:11:55.000 --> 00:11:58.300
So when Google Cloud was
first getting created, or even

00:11:58.300 --> 00:12:00.790
before Google Cloud, when there
were just Google Developer

00:12:00.790 --> 00:12:05.230
APIs, we relied on access
scopes as basically identity

00:12:05.230 --> 00:12:06.260
and access management.

00:12:06.260 --> 00:12:08.380
So in order to
control whether or not

00:12:08.380 --> 00:12:10.540
you had permission to
read from a Cloud Storage

00:12:10.540 --> 00:12:12.850
bucket versus writing to
a Cloud Storage bucket,

00:12:12.850 --> 00:12:15.100
you had different access scopes.

00:12:15.100 --> 00:12:17.787
But there are some limitations
on the specification.

00:12:17.787 --> 00:12:19.120
I won't go into a lot of detail.

00:12:19.120 --> 00:12:21.370
But we've since evolved
Google Cloud platform

00:12:21.370 --> 00:12:24.130
to have a really, really
rigorous identity and access

00:12:24.130 --> 00:12:25.490
management system.

00:12:25.490 --> 00:12:28.510
So in general, when you're
interacting with Google Cloud,

00:12:28.510 --> 00:12:32.110
we recommend that you grant
the Cloud platform OAuth scope

00:12:32.110 --> 00:12:34.360
to the account that's
being authorized,

00:12:34.360 --> 00:12:36.580
and then you leverage
identity and access management

00:12:36.580 --> 00:12:39.460
to give really fine-grained
roles and permissions that

00:12:39.460 --> 00:12:42.118
follow the principle
of least privilege.

00:12:42.118 --> 00:12:43.410
STEPHANIE WONG: Got it, got it.

00:12:43.410 --> 00:12:46.020
And if I recall
correctly, I think

00:12:46.020 --> 00:12:49.770
there might be a case where if
you're using a default service

00:12:49.770 --> 00:12:53.550
account, it still has
access scopes without IAM.

00:12:53.550 --> 00:12:54.522
Is that true?

00:12:54.522 --> 00:12:55.230
SETH VARGO: Yeah.

00:12:55.230 --> 00:12:58.020
So if you're running on Google
Compute Engine or Google

00:12:58.020 --> 00:13:01.800
Kubernetes Engine, there is
a default service account

00:13:01.800 --> 00:13:04.680
that has some pretty
restrictive access scopes.

00:13:04.680 --> 00:13:07.830
So when you actually provision a
virtual machine or a Kubernetes

00:13:07.830 --> 00:13:09.810
cluster, you get
to pick-- do you

00:13:09.810 --> 00:13:13.440
want access to all Cloud
APIs, the default APIs,

00:13:13.440 --> 00:13:15.510
or none, which is no scopes?

00:13:15.510 --> 00:13:18.150
And in general,
you should probably

00:13:18.150 --> 00:13:20.130
click the box that
says all APIs,

00:13:20.130 --> 00:13:22.950
and then use identity and
access management instead

00:13:22.950 --> 00:13:25.300
to practice the principle
of least privilege.

00:13:25.300 --> 00:13:27.330
But for backwards
compatibility reasons,

00:13:27.330 --> 00:13:30.840
or for workloads that have
existed for 10 or 20 years,

00:13:30.840 --> 00:13:33.390
we preserve the default
behavior for the default service

00:13:33.390 --> 00:13:35.580
account, which does use
those restricted access

00:13:35.580 --> 00:13:37.623
scopes as a way to do ACLs.

00:13:37.623 --> 00:13:38.790
STEPHANIE WONG: Makes sense.

00:13:38.790 --> 00:13:39.420
OK.

00:13:39.420 --> 00:13:42.840
So moving on to our
next question, and this

00:13:42.840 --> 00:13:46.290
is from Guillaume
B., who asks, when

00:13:46.290 --> 00:13:48.510
should I use a service
account key file?

00:13:48.510 --> 00:13:50.160
Or when can I avoid it?

00:13:50.160 --> 00:13:52.600
On prem, locally, on GCP?

00:13:52.600 --> 00:13:54.342
So Seth, can you
answer this one for us?

00:13:54.342 --> 00:13:55.050
SETH VARGO: Yeah.

00:13:55.050 --> 00:13:57.342
This is a really funny
question, because Guillame and I

00:13:57.342 --> 00:14:00.605
have actually talked about
this offline at length.

00:14:00.605 --> 00:14:01.980
Real quick, I do
want to clarify,

00:14:01.980 --> 00:14:04.410
what Guillame is asking
is about a service account

00:14:04.410 --> 00:14:06.760
key, which is different
than a service account.

00:14:06.760 --> 00:14:08.760
So Carter already gave a
really good explanation

00:14:08.760 --> 00:14:09.720
of a service account.

00:14:09.720 --> 00:14:14.160
It's basically a robot that
can authenticate like a human.

00:14:14.160 --> 00:14:15.660
As a human, you
tend to authenticate

00:14:15.660 --> 00:14:16.900
with like a password.

00:14:16.900 --> 00:14:20.010
Well, a service account
doesn't have a password.

00:14:20.010 --> 00:14:21.580
It's usually just
an email address.

00:14:21.580 --> 00:14:22.470
So the way that you--

00:14:22.470 --> 00:14:25.890
I'll use air quotes-- "log
in" as a service account is

00:14:25.890 --> 00:14:27.900
with the service account key.

00:14:27.900 --> 00:14:29.100
It's basically the password.

00:14:29.100 --> 00:14:32.250
And anyone who has that key
can authenticate and act

00:14:32.250 --> 00:14:33.790
as that service account.

00:14:33.790 --> 00:14:35.600
So to directly
answer the question,

00:14:35.600 --> 00:14:37.350
when should I use the
service account key,

00:14:37.350 --> 00:14:39.330
the answer is never.

00:14:39.330 --> 00:14:41.190
You should use
service account keys

00:14:41.190 --> 00:14:45.570
as infrequently as possible,
because you convert an identity

00:14:45.570 --> 00:14:47.872
management problem into a
secrets management problem.

00:14:47.872 --> 00:14:49.330
Where are you going
to put the key?

00:14:49.330 --> 00:14:50.622
How are you going to rotate it?

00:14:50.622 --> 00:14:52.510
How are you going to
audit access to it?

00:14:52.510 --> 00:14:55.560
What happens if it gets in the
hands of an unauthorized party?

00:14:55.560 --> 00:14:59.190
So in general, you should
use service accounts

00:14:59.190 --> 00:15:01.840
or service account impersonation
as much as possible.

00:15:01.840 --> 00:15:03.577
So let's say you're
working locally,

00:15:03.577 --> 00:15:05.160
whether you're
building an application

00:15:05.160 --> 00:15:07.290
or you're running
something like Terraform.

00:15:07.290 --> 00:15:10.230
You should authenticate with
gcloud or the Cloud SDK.

00:15:10.230 --> 00:15:12.930
When you do this, your
local human authentication

00:15:12.930 --> 00:15:16.120
credentials will automatically
be picked up by those tools.

00:15:16.120 --> 00:15:19.350
Similarly, if you're using
any client library that Google

00:15:19.350 --> 00:15:21.462
Cloud publishes
or any SDK, we'll

00:15:21.462 --> 00:15:23.670
automatically use what are
called default application

00:15:23.670 --> 00:15:25.470
credentials to pull
the credentials

00:15:25.470 --> 00:15:27.520
from your local machine.

00:15:27.520 --> 00:15:29.700
If you're running
on Google Cloud,

00:15:29.700 --> 00:15:31.890
Cloud Run, GCE,
Kubernetes, whatever,

00:15:31.890 --> 00:15:34.950
you can use the machine identity
or the workload identity

00:15:34.950 --> 00:15:38.080
to attach a service account
specifically to the workload.

00:15:38.080 --> 00:15:40.440
So for Cloud Run
or GCE, you pick

00:15:40.440 --> 00:15:42.690
which service account you
want to use when you spin up

00:15:42.690 --> 00:15:43.980
the service or the VM.

00:15:43.980 --> 00:15:45.782
For, say, Kubernetes,
you actually

00:15:45.782 --> 00:15:48.240
use workload identity to map
the Kubernetes service account

00:15:48.240 --> 00:15:50.130
to a GCP service account.

00:15:50.130 --> 00:15:51.630
In all of these
situations, you're

00:15:51.630 --> 00:15:54.270
still using service accounts,
but there's no key exchange.

00:15:54.270 --> 00:15:57.540
We're relying on Google
Cloud to provide the identity

00:15:57.540 --> 00:15:58.320
to the service--

00:15:58.320 --> 00:16:00.070
specifically, the
metadata service, if you

00:16:00.070 --> 00:16:02.468
want to get into some detail.

00:16:02.468 --> 00:16:05.010
But then you might ask yourself,
well, what if I multi-cloud?

00:16:05.010 --> 00:16:07.230
What if I'm running on
another cloud provider?

00:16:07.230 --> 00:16:08.670
How do I authenticate that?

00:16:08.670 --> 00:16:12.300
Well, if you're running
on, say, AWS or Azure,

00:16:12.300 --> 00:16:15.360
you can use workload
identity federation-- or WIF,

00:16:15.360 --> 00:16:18.960
as I like to call it-- to
basically delegate trust.

00:16:18.960 --> 00:16:23.670
You can use information from,
say, that other cloud provider,

00:16:23.670 --> 00:16:26.100
and present that as an
attestation to Google Cloud.

00:16:26.100 --> 00:16:27.810
And assuming that
you've configured

00:16:27.810 --> 00:16:30.240
it to allow that attestation,
you can exchange,

00:16:30.240 --> 00:16:33.030
say, your AWS credential
for a GCP credential--

00:16:33.030 --> 00:16:36.940
again, never actually exporting
a JSON service account key.

00:16:36.940 --> 00:16:40.200
So that really leaves
the on-premise scenario,

00:16:40.200 --> 00:16:45.120
where you don't have an identity
provider like Active Directory,

00:16:45.120 --> 00:16:48.340
or you have some collection
of disparate systems.

00:16:48.340 --> 00:16:49.830
This is really
the only situation

00:16:49.830 --> 00:16:52.830
in which you would need to
export a service account key,

00:16:52.830 --> 00:16:54.630
where you don't have
what we call an IDP

00:16:54.630 --> 00:16:57.870
or an identity provider
that is OIDC-compliant.

00:16:57.870 --> 00:16:59.580
In those situations,
unfortunately,

00:16:59.580 --> 00:17:02.130
today, the only option is to
export a service account key.

00:17:02.130 --> 00:17:04.800
But in general,
especially working locally

00:17:04.800 --> 00:17:07.680
on your laptop or anything
that you run on Google Cloud,

00:17:07.680 --> 00:17:10.200
you should be able to do
so without exporting a JSON

00:17:10.200 --> 00:17:11.380
service account key.

00:17:11.380 --> 00:17:14.579
And in fact, if you work
for a large enterprise,

00:17:14.579 --> 00:17:16.950
your company might have
an organization policy

00:17:16.950 --> 00:17:19.109
that prevents you from
downloading those service

00:17:19.109 --> 00:17:21.569
account keys, which we
can talk about more maybe

00:17:21.569 --> 00:17:24.300
in a future episode
on org policies,

00:17:24.300 --> 00:17:26.160
because that's at least
a 30-minute episode

00:17:26.160 --> 00:17:27.055
all on its own.

00:17:27.055 --> 00:17:27.930
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah.

00:17:27.930 --> 00:17:30.130
This can branch out all
sorts of directions.

00:17:30.130 --> 00:17:32.910
But to sum it up, as you
said, so best practices

00:17:32.910 --> 00:17:35.280
is use attached service
accounts whenever possible,

00:17:35.280 --> 00:17:38.970
or workload identity to attach
service accounts to Kubernetes

00:17:38.970 --> 00:17:39.570
pods.

00:17:39.570 --> 00:17:42.330
Or you said WIF, workload
identity federation,

00:17:42.330 --> 00:17:45.080
to let applications running
on-premise or other clouds

00:17:45.080 --> 00:17:49.200
to use a service account,
and using service account

00:17:49.200 --> 00:17:51.150
keys if there's no
viable alternative,

00:17:51.150 --> 00:17:55.690
so only when it's sort of
the last possible scenario.

00:17:55.690 --> 00:17:56.190
All right.

00:17:56.190 --> 00:17:58.770
So let's go ahead and move
on to the next question.

00:17:58.770 --> 00:18:03.160
And this is from
Ettan2007, who asks,

00:18:03.160 --> 00:18:06.420
how do you guard against
one or more rogue employees?

00:18:06.420 --> 00:18:09.630
And this is talking about at
one of Google's data centers.

00:18:09.630 --> 00:18:11.892
So Carter, let's go to you
for this tough question.

00:18:11.892 --> 00:18:13.350
CARTER MORGAN: For
this tough one--

00:18:13.350 --> 00:18:16.630
if there's a gaggle of rogue
Googlers, what do we do?

00:18:16.630 --> 00:18:21.150
Thank you, Ettan2007,
for this one.

00:18:21.150 --> 00:18:23.130
There's really a lot
of different ways

00:18:23.130 --> 00:18:23.950
that this happens.

00:18:23.950 --> 00:18:26.100
Seth and Stephanie
already talked about this,

00:18:26.100 --> 00:18:30.090
but Google has this thing
called defense in depth.

00:18:30.090 --> 00:18:32.500
There's a lot of different
measures in place.

00:18:32.500 --> 00:18:34.950
So some of those
measures are limiting

00:18:34.950 --> 00:18:36.370
who has access to what.

00:18:36.370 --> 00:18:38.010
So example-- and
I think I saw this

00:18:38.010 --> 00:18:40.338
in Stephanie's
amazing data center

00:18:40.338 --> 00:18:42.630
video, which, if you haven't
checked it out, go see it.

00:18:42.630 --> 00:18:43.530
It's amazing.

00:18:43.530 --> 00:18:47.070
But less than 1% of Googlers
have access to the data center.

00:18:47.070 --> 00:18:49.597
So I don't know where
the data centers are.

00:18:49.597 --> 00:18:51.180
I couldn't go log
in to one right now.

00:18:51.180 --> 00:18:53.610
So that takes me
out of the picture.

00:18:53.610 --> 00:18:55.950
Two, there's a lot of
training and policies on who

00:18:55.950 --> 00:18:58.830
can access what and when, like
when is it OK to access data?

00:18:58.830 --> 00:19:00.810
And so we get a lot
of training on that.

00:19:00.810 --> 00:19:02.987
And then Stephanie
mentioned earlier about

00:19:02.987 --> 00:19:05.070
how you can pair something
you have with something

00:19:05.070 --> 00:19:06.840
you know with something you are.

00:19:06.840 --> 00:19:09.120
And these are examples
of defense in depth.

00:19:09.120 --> 00:19:11.010
And this applies to
the data center, too.

00:19:11.010 --> 00:19:14.130
So there's scanners to
say, hmm, who are you?

00:19:14.130 --> 00:19:14.970
Should you be here?

00:19:14.970 --> 00:19:16.803
And if you shouldn't,
you can't, supposedly,

00:19:16.803 --> 00:19:19.823
get access to what's
inside the data center.

00:19:19.823 --> 00:19:21.240
So there's a lot
of different ways

00:19:21.240 --> 00:19:22.865
and a lot of different
defense measures

00:19:22.865 --> 00:19:25.415
that Google has in place to
protect the data centers.

00:19:25.415 --> 00:19:27.540
And I think Stephanie can
go into a lot more detail

00:19:27.540 --> 00:19:29.665
here, because you made this
amazing video about all

00:19:29.665 --> 00:19:30.990
of this.

00:19:30.990 --> 00:19:31.920
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah.

00:19:31.920 --> 00:19:35.222
Well, actually, Seth, do
you want to cover it first?

00:19:35.222 --> 00:19:35.930
SETH VARGO: Yeah.

00:19:35.930 --> 00:19:37.670
So I'll let you talk
about the data centers,

00:19:37.670 --> 00:19:39.170
since you're far more
knowledgeable there.

00:19:39.170 --> 00:19:40.670
I'll talk a little
bit about, maybe,

00:19:40.670 --> 00:19:43.200
Google's internal
controls that we have.

00:19:43.200 --> 00:19:45.200
So one of the things that
you always worry about

00:19:45.200 --> 00:19:47.770
is like one employee
going off and downloading

00:19:47.770 --> 00:19:49.520
a whole bunch of data
or looking at things

00:19:49.520 --> 00:19:50.687
that they shouldn't look at.

00:19:50.687 --> 00:19:52.790
And internally at
Google, we have a number

00:19:52.790 --> 00:19:55.310
of controls and
systems in place that

00:19:55.310 --> 00:19:56.880
enforce kind of three things.

00:19:56.880 --> 00:19:58.428
They enforce your
authentication,

00:19:58.428 --> 00:19:59.720
so are you who you say you are?

00:19:59.720 --> 00:20:01.637
We already talked around
context-aware access.

00:20:01.637 --> 00:20:04.310
We have custom-built
hardware for doing

00:20:04.310 --> 00:20:06.680
multifactor authentication
using security keys.

00:20:06.680 --> 00:20:10.160
So let's just say that's
kind of a solved problem.

00:20:10.160 --> 00:20:13.340
Then we have, do you have
cause for accessing this data?

00:20:13.340 --> 00:20:16.670
So Googlers can't
just go look at data.

00:20:16.670 --> 00:20:18.290
There needs to be
probable cause, kind

00:20:18.290 --> 00:20:19.790
of like in a courtroom, almost.

00:20:19.790 --> 00:20:22.670
That cause could be a
customer support ticket.

00:20:22.670 --> 00:20:25.220
It could be a production outage.

00:20:25.220 --> 00:20:27.920
There's a number of safeguards,
maybe like 20 of them.

00:20:27.920 --> 00:20:30.110
But whenever I'm trying
to access user data,

00:20:30.110 --> 00:20:32.300
or any Googler is trying
to access user data,

00:20:32.300 --> 00:20:35.210
they have to provide
some justification.

00:20:35.210 --> 00:20:37.488
And that justification,
again, can come

00:20:37.488 --> 00:20:38.780
from a number of other sources.

00:20:38.780 --> 00:20:41.330
It could be customer-initiated,
or it could be from an outage.

00:20:41.330 --> 00:20:42.955
And then the third
piece of information

00:20:42.955 --> 00:20:45.080
they have to provide
or go through

00:20:45.080 --> 00:20:47.270
is usually some
multi-party approval.

00:20:47.270 --> 00:20:50.815
So even if there's a
support case, in order

00:20:50.815 --> 00:20:52.190
to go look at the
user data, they

00:20:52.190 --> 00:20:55.370
would need someone else on their
team or their sister SRE team

00:20:55.370 --> 00:20:57.050
to approve that request.

00:20:57.050 --> 00:20:58.640
That request is time-limited.

00:20:58.640 --> 00:21:00.620
They may only have that
authorization for, say,

00:21:00.620 --> 00:21:02.630
30 minutes or an
hour until they need

00:21:02.630 --> 00:21:04.190
to renew that authorization.

00:21:04.190 --> 00:21:07.760
So multi-party approvals
and justifications, and then

00:21:07.760 --> 00:21:10.800
an underlying audit log
that tracks all of this.

00:21:10.800 --> 00:21:14.410
So even if someone goes
through all the processes

00:21:14.410 --> 00:21:16.160
and approvals, there's
still an audit log

00:21:16.160 --> 00:21:18.710
under the hood that demonstrates
the steps they took,

00:21:18.710 --> 00:21:22.435
who approved it, what tickets
or tickets was it attached to.

00:21:22.435 --> 00:21:23.810
And then ultimately,
that can get

00:21:23.810 --> 00:21:25.880
surfaced in our access
transparency report, which

00:21:25.880 --> 00:21:29.452
is a Google Cloud product, where
you can see if a Googler has

00:21:29.452 --> 00:21:31.160
accessed your data,
why they accessed it,

00:21:31.160 --> 00:21:33.655
and what approvals they
went through to do so.

00:21:33.655 --> 00:21:34.530
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah.

00:21:34.530 --> 00:21:37.680
It's all about reducing insider
risk through automation,

00:21:37.680 --> 00:21:39.870
doing things in a very
safe and controlled way

00:21:39.870 --> 00:21:42.660
with the two-party approvals
and the limited APIs

00:21:42.660 --> 00:21:46.140
so that we don't expose
any sensitive information.

00:21:46.140 --> 00:21:49.050
But yeah, same thing goes
for Google employees' access

00:21:49.050 --> 00:21:52.740
to any end-user information
through access transparency,

00:21:52.740 --> 00:21:53.460
as you mentioned.

00:21:53.460 --> 00:21:55.230
So there's a
security white paper

00:21:55.230 --> 00:21:57.330
that you all should check out.

00:21:57.330 --> 00:21:59.620
Lots of stuff that Seth
has worked on as well.

00:21:59.620 --> 00:22:02.363
But to just touch on the data
center experience that I have,

00:22:02.363 --> 00:22:04.530
I can tell you from experience
it was very difficult

00:22:04.530 --> 00:22:06.912
for me to even be able to be
in there in the first place.

00:22:06.912 --> 00:22:08.370
But ACLs are
important, so you have

00:22:08.370 --> 00:22:12.240
to be on an access control list
to even be authorized to enter.

00:22:12.240 --> 00:22:15.540
There is, as I said, the
badge readers everywhere.

00:22:15.540 --> 00:22:18.900
There is biometric scanning at
certain locations in the data

00:22:18.900 --> 00:22:21.790
center in particular, especially
the data center floor.

00:22:21.790 --> 00:22:26.010
So lots of measures in place
to prevent rogue employees

00:22:26.010 --> 00:22:29.220
as well as anyone
trying to leave the data

00:22:29.220 --> 00:22:31.110
center by bringing anything out.

00:22:31.110 --> 00:22:34.870
There is metal detectors as you
leave certain areas as well.

00:22:34.870 --> 00:22:35.370
So--

00:22:35.370 --> 00:22:36.900
SETH VARGO: And if I could
just chime in real fast.

00:22:36.900 --> 00:22:37.775
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah.

00:22:37.775 --> 00:22:40.260
SETH VARGO: We don't just do
those things for insider risk,

00:22:40.260 --> 00:22:40.780
as well.

00:22:40.780 --> 00:22:43.230
So I know it sounds a
little Big-Brothery,

00:22:43.230 --> 00:22:45.840
but I wanted to add an
additional color on why

00:22:45.840 --> 00:22:47.357
this infrastructure exists.

00:22:47.357 --> 00:22:49.440
And we'll probably get to
this a little bit later.

00:22:49.440 --> 00:22:51.660
But if there was ever, say,
a fire at a data center,

00:22:51.660 --> 00:22:54.570
it's really important that
Google's global security team

00:22:54.570 --> 00:22:56.250
knows who is in
that data center so

00:22:56.250 --> 00:22:58.500
that we can ensure that
everyone was evacuated safely.

00:22:58.500 --> 00:23:00.060
The same goes for, say,
a physical building.

00:23:00.060 --> 00:23:02.130
If there was a natural
disaster, like an earthquake,

00:23:02.130 --> 00:23:03.780
we need to make sure that
all of those Googlers

00:23:03.780 --> 00:23:04.990
are safe and accounted for.

00:23:04.990 --> 00:23:07.740
So it's not purely about
preventing insider risk.

00:23:07.740 --> 00:23:09.610
That's obviously
one of the benefits.

00:23:09.610 --> 00:23:11.970
But this data
exists for a number

00:23:11.970 --> 00:23:13.950
of internal systems,
a lot of which

00:23:13.950 --> 00:23:16.515
are designed to help
keep Googlers safe.

00:23:16.515 --> 00:23:17.640
STEPHANIE WONG: Absolutely.

00:23:17.640 --> 00:23:19.420
That's important to
underline as well.

00:23:19.420 --> 00:23:22.240
And I'll talk a little bit more
about that in just a moment.

00:23:22.240 --> 00:23:24.360
But we do have a
related question.

00:23:24.360 --> 00:23:27.160
And this is from
David Adams, who asks,

00:23:27.160 --> 00:23:29.940
I wonder if all data centers
have the identical six

00:23:29.940 --> 00:23:33.720
layers of security, or if this
is just your flagship data

00:23:33.720 --> 00:23:34.770
center?

00:23:34.770 --> 00:23:36.990
This is probably a
question from the video

00:23:36.990 --> 00:23:38.790
that I had created
about the six layers.

00:23:38.790 --> 00:23:40.980
And this is related
to, also, the concept

00:23:40.980 --> 00:23:42.640
of defense in depth.

00:23:42.640 --> 00:23:45.640
So I'm going to go ahead and
actually answer this one.

00:23:45.640 --> 00:23:48.060
But yes, all of
our data centers do

00:23:48.060 --> 00:23:51.150
have and follow the same
layered approach of security

00:23:51.150 --> 00:23:52.450
at this time.

00:23:52.450 --> 00:23:54.930
So if you watch the video,
which you can check out

00:23:54.930 --> 00:23:59.370
at bit.ly/6layersdeep, you'll
learn that layers become

00:23:59.370 --> 00:24:02.100
progressively more protected
with access checks as you move

00:24:02.100 --> 00:24:03.730
to the core of the data center.

00:24:03.730 --> 00:24:06.300
So you have layer 1, which
is the exterior, the security

00:24:06.300 --> 00:24:08.100
perimeter, the
property boundaries.

00:24:08.100 --> 00:24:10.290
And then layer 2 is
the main entrance gate.

00:24:10.290 --> 00:24:12.540
So that has a vehicle
crash barrier,

00:24:12.540 --> 00:24:16.500
fences with motion detection,
guard kiosks, thermal cameras.

00:24:16.500 --> 00:24:19.410
Layer 3 is building access,
with the general office

00:24:19.410 --> 00:24:22.080
area where our hardware
and operations team work.

00:24:22.080 --> 00:24:23.940
It actually looks just
like a regular Google

00:24:23.940 --> 00:24:25.770
office in this section, too.

00:24:25.770 --> 00:24:28.290
But it also includes an
isolated loading dock area

00:24:28.290 --> 00:24:31.110
where secure shipments
are made from vendors

00:24:31.110 --> 00:24:33.660
that we have already approved.

00:24:33.660 --> 00:24:36.340
Layer 4, inside the
data center itself.

00:24:36.340 --> 00:24:38.610
It includes operational
rooms like the core network

00:24:38.610 --> 00:24:41.590
room and the security
operation center.

00:24:41.590 --> 00:24:44.880
Now, this is very related to
what Seth was just mentioning.

00:24:44.880 --> 00:24:47.190
The SOC contains a
highly trained staff

00:24:47.190 --> 00:24:49.590
that monitors all
aspects of security.

00:24:49.590 --> 00:24:51.960
And they're responsible
for keeping a level

00:24:51.960 --> 00:24:53.130
head at all times--

00:24:53.130 --> 00:24:56.220
for example, during
these major events,

00:24:56.220 --> 00:24:59.370
or if there's a natural
disaster-- to keep people safe.

00:24:59.370 --> 00:25:01.650
Layer 5 is the
data center floor.

00:25:01.650 --> 00:25:04.140
And that includes
multifactor authentication

00:25:04.140 --> 00:25:08.100
like badge readers,
biometric scans, et cetera.

00:25:08.100 --> 00:25:10.420
It's the most protected area.

00:25:10.420 --> 00:25:13.230
And it also includes
layer 6, which is inside.

00:25:13.230 --> 00:25:14.850
And that's disk arrays.

00:25:14.850 --> 00:25:17.790
So once again, go
ahead and check out

00:25:17.790 --> 00:25:19.890
the video to get a
full experience of what

00:25:19.890 --> 00:25:22.008
it was like to go through that.

00:25:22.008 --> 00:25:23.800
All right, so moving
on-- our next question

00:25:23.800 --> 00:25:25.720
is actually a question
we have received

00:25:25.720 --> 00:25:30.100
from a lot of our viewers of
our security content on YouTube.

00:25:30.100 --> 00:25:35.470
And that is, why does Google
destroy hard drives at its data

00:25:35.470 --> 00:25:38.230
centers instead of
overwriting them?

00:25:38.230 --> 00:25:41.380
Now, I just talked about disk
arrays, which is layer 6.

00:25:41.380 --> 00:25:43.042
But let's start
with you, Carter,

00:25:43.042 --> 00:25:44.125
for this popular question.

00:25:44.125 --> 00:25:46.667
CARTER MORGAN: Oh, I love it,
because this is a question I've

00:25:46.667 --> 00:25:48.700
seen even on series I've made.

00:25:48.700 --> 00:25:50.612
And I think, to
answer this, I want

00:25:50.612 --> 00:25:52.570
to first touch back to
some of the things we've

00:25:52.570 --> 00:25:53.830
talked about here.

00:25:53.830 --> 00:25:56.620
Google is constantly
taking multiple steps

00:25:56.620 --> 00:25:59.060
to secure our data.

00:25:59.060 --> 00:26:01.040
And this is the
same kind of thing.

00:26:01.040 --> 00:26:04.660
So if someone were to take a
drive, that data is encrypted.

00:26:04.660 --> 00:26:06.220
Sometimes it's
hardware-encrypted,

00:26:06.220 --> 00:26:08.380
where there is specific
chips on a device

00:26:08.380 --> 00:26:10.030
to encrypt and decrypt it.

00:26:10.030 --> 00:26:12.353
But if someone were to
actually have a physical drive,

00:26:12.353 --> 00:26:14.770
they have something-- they
have an advantage, a very large

00:26:14.770 --> 00:26:16.018
advantage, and that's time.

00:26:16.018 --> 00:26:17.560
And so they can
brute-force, and they

00:26:17.560 --> 00:26:20.300
might be able to figure
out ways to access that.

00:26:20.300 --> 00:26:22.960
So destroying that
is another step

00:26:22.960 --> 00:26:24.560
to prevent things like that.

00:26:24.560 --> 00:26:26.170
And then something
I hadn't realized--

00:26:26.170 --> 00:26:27.670
when I was talking
with Seth before this,

00:26:27.670 --> 00:26:30.070
we were running through some
of the questions and whatnot.

00:26:30.070 --> 00:26:31.240
And he was like,
yeah, well, have

00:26:31.240 --> 00:26:32.740
you thought about
future technology,

00:26:32.740 --> 00:26:35.260
and how future technology
might be able to do things

00:26:35.260 --> 00:26:36.520
that we can't right now?

00:26:36.520 --> 00:26:39.550
And so this is another way
to prevent against that.

00:26:39.550 --> 00:26:43.000
Sure, the encryptions
might be very safe now,

00:26:43.000 --> 00:26:45.800
with today's technology,
but what about in 20 years?

00:26:45.800 --> 00:26:49.450
And so this is just another step
for ensuring that our data is

00:26:49.450 --> 00:26:51.185
safe and secure on Google.

00:26:51.185 --> 00:26:52.060
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah.

00:26:52.060 --> 00:26:54.302
Seth, do you have
anything to add there?

00:26:54.302 --> 00:26:55.010
SETH VARGO: Yeah.

00:26:55.010 --> 00:26:57.510
I'll just draw a quick
analogy, because Carter kind of

00:26:57.510 --> 00:26:58.510
explained it eloquently.

00:26:58.510 --> 00:27:01.420
But people love to use the
word post-quantum crypto,

00:27:01.420 --> 00:27:02.410
so I won't go there.

00:27:02.410 --> 00:27:06.160
What I will say is let's
talk about DNA, right?

00:27:06.160 --> 00:27:10.000
DNA is an important part
of every criminal case

00:27:10.000 --> 00:27:11.020
that you see today.

00:27:11.020 --> 00:27:13.450
But 20 years ago, no
one talked about DNA.

00:27:13.450 --> 00:27:16.330
It was all fingerprint--
loops and hooks.

00:27:16.330 --> 00:27:18.640
And fingerprint evidence
is still important,

00:27:18.640 --> 00:27:22.090
but we've made pretty
significant advancements

00:27:22.090 --> 00:27:24.400
in the biomedical space
with respect to DNA

00:27:24.400 --> 00:27:27.153
and how we can use
DNA in criminal cases.

00:27:27.153 --> 00:27:29.320
You can imagine that something
similar will probably

00:27:29.320 --> 00:27:32.350
happen in 20 years with
respect to data recovery.

00:27:32.350 --> 00:27:34.240
And that's really
one of the reasons

00:27:34.240 --> 00:27:36.370
why we're investing so
heavily in this space

00:27:36.370 --> 00:27:40.320
and why we do truly shred,
physically, these hard drives--

00:27:40.320 --> 00:27:42.070
because we don't want
to be in a situation

00:27:42.070 --> 00:27:44.440
where someone grabs one
of these hard drives,

00:27:44.440 --> 00:27:46.900
puts it on a shelf for 20
years, and then there's

00:27:46.900 --> 00:27:50.350
this magical new scanner
that you put over it

00:27:50.350 --> 00:27:53.530
and it extracts all of the
data as if it was in plaintext.

00:27:53.530 --> 00:27:55.968
And physically destroying
the drives is really, again,

00:27:55.968 --> 00:27:57.010
part of defense in depth.

00:27:57.010 --> 00:27:59.080
It's one of the ways that
we can truly prevent that

00:27:59.080 --> 00:28:01.538
from happening, no matter what
advancements in technologies

00:28:01.538 --> 00:28:03.005
might exist in the future.

00:28:03.005 --> 00:28:03.880
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah.

00:28:03.880 --> 00:28:05.440
I think it's also
important to note

00:28:05.440 --> 00:28:07.990
that we do try to recycle
drives and their components.

00:28:07.990 --> 00:28:10.900
But in the case that a
drive cannot be erased,

00:28:10.900 --> 00:28:15.010
then it is stored securely until
it can be physically destroyed.

00:28:15.010 --> 00:28:17.290
And if you're wondering
what happens to this waste,

00:28:17.290 --> 00:28:20.050
we do have researchers
in our Oklahoma facility

00:28:20.050 --> 00:28:22.900
who are looking into methods
of collecting and recycling

00:28:22.900 --> 00:28:26.080
these rare earth
metals in hard drives.

00:28:26.080 --> 00:28:28.930
As you might know, data centers
are the largest consumers

00:28:28.930 --> 00:28:31.750
of these hard
drives that contain

00:28:31.750 --> 00:28:35.350
these elements like dysprosium,
and so these elements

00:28:35.350 --> 00:28:38.350
have a major role in components
of things like electric cars

00:28:38.350 --> 00:28:39.550
and wind turbines.

00:28:39.550 --> 00:28:42.683
And so we're doing a lot there
to make better use of them.

00:28:42.683 --> 00:28:44.350
And there's a great
article to check out

00:28:44.350 --> 00:28:46.430
if you want to learn
more about that.

00:28:46.430 --> 00:28:46.930
All right.

00:28:46.930 --> 00:28:51.220
So our next question comes
from Oliver M., who asks,

00:28:51.220 --> 00:28:53.830
why is there so much
physical security

00:28:53.830 --> 00:28:55.210
at Google data centers?

00:28:55.210 --> 00:28:57.340
And is there any
reason to believe

00:28:57.340 --> 00:28:59.140
that people might
want to destroy

00:28:59.140 --> 00:29:00.970
the data or the servers?

00:29:00.970 --> 00:29:04.590
So Seth, I'm going to
throw this one at you.

00:29:04.590 --> 00:29:08.115
SETH VARGO: Yeah, so
this is a great one.

00:29:08.115 --> 00:29:09.490
It's kind of a
two-part question.

00:29:09.490 --> 00:29:11.330
So Carter already
answered the first part,

00:29:11.330 --> 00:29:13.450
which is once you
have physical access

00:29:13.450 --> 00:29:18.640
to a device or a machine or a
hard drive, a lot of the bets

00:29:18.640 --> 00:29:19.450
are off, right?

00:29:19.450 --> 00:29:22.450
If I can run a scan
on those hard drive,

00:29:22.450 --> 00:29:25.880
if I can try to brute-force
the various decryption keys,

00:29:25.880 --> 00:29:31.228
if I can plug a thumb drive
in, I can do a lot more damage.

00:29:31.228 --> 00:29:33.520
To draw an analogy, not
thinking about the data center,

00:29:33.520 --> 00:29:36.430
if I have a Wi-Fi access
point in my building,

00:29:36.430 --> 00:29:38.620
I can't do much with
that access point

00:29:38.620 --> 00:29:39.710
if it's up on the ceiling.

00:29:39.710 --> 00:29:41.290
But if I can get
it down on my desk,

00:29:41.290 --> 00:29:44.170
I can stick a paperclip
into the Reset button.

00:29:44.170 --> 00:29:46.000
And I can reset it,
I can provision it

00:29:46.000 --> 00:29:49.120
with my own custom firmware,
I can directly connect to it.

00:29:49.120 --> 00:29:50.457
The threat model changes.

00:29:50.457 --> 00:29:52.540
The threat model becomes
very difficult to protect

00:29:52.540 --> 00:29:53.360
at this point.

00:29:53.360 --> 00:29:54.940
And we do have
protections in place.

00:29:54.940 --> 00:29:58.720
We have secure boot mechanisms,
and the TPMs that make sure

00:29:58.720 --> 00:30:00.280
that the hard drive
and the machine

00:30:00.280 --> 00:30:01.490
hasn't been tampered with.

00:30:01.490 --> 00:30:04.850
But it's much harder to
protect at that layer.

00:30:04.850 --> 00:30:07.578
So that's why physical
security is so important.

00:30:07.578 --> 00:30:09.370
And then to your second
question, is like--

00:30:09.370 --> 00:30:11.952
are people trying to break
into Google data centers?

00:30:11.952 --> 00:30:13.660
I mean, we can't go
into a ton of detail,

00:30:13.660 --> 00:30:16.265
but the answer is not no.

00:30:16.265 --> 00:30:17.890
And the answer is
not unique to Google.

00:30:17.890 --> 00:30:18.730
Right?

00:30:18.730 --> 00:30:21.160
We know that customers
store data in data centers.

00:30:21.160 --> 00:30:23.410
Even what we call colo
data centers, which

00:30:23.410 --> 00:30:25.600
are data centers
as a service, much

00:30:25.600 --> 00:30:27.430
smaller than a public
cloud provider--

00:30:27.430 --> 00:30:29.260
they have the same
levels of security,

00:30:29.260 --> 00:30:33.670
or similar levels of security,
because this data is important.

00:30:33.670 --> 00:30:36.370
Customers are trusting
Google to secure their data.

00:30:36.370 --> 00:30:39.160
If we do have a data
breach, it erodes the trust

00:30:39.160 --> 00:30:43.270
that customers have in
securing their data with us.

00:30:43.270 --> 00:30:45.160
So this is why
it's so important.

00:30:45.160 --> 00:30:49.358
And just yesterday,
and the day before,

00:30:49.358 --> 00:30:51.400
and the day before that--
in the past three days,

00:30:51.400 --> 00:30:54.280
there have been three
major independent security

00:30:54.280 --> 00:30:57.460
breaches targeting companies
that are of the size of Google.

00:30:57.460 --> 00:30:59.220
So it's really not
out of the question

00:30:59.220 --> 00:31:01.720
that people are trying to gain
access to these data centers,

00:31:01.720 --> 00:31:03.967
and that's why we have
these protections in place.

00:31:03.967 --> 00:31:05.050
STEPHANIE WONG: All right.

00:31:05.050 --> 00:31:07.780
Well, here is another
question that we have received

00:31:07.780 --> 00:31:10.090
from many of our viewers--

00:31:10.090 --> 00:31:12.640
what if a data center
catches on fire?

00:31:12.640 --> 00:31:15.470
Would everybody in the
world lose their data?

00:31:15.470 --> 00:31:17.890
So this is one of my favorite
questions that we get.

00:31:17.890 --> 00:31:20.060
So Carter, can you help
us out with this one?

00:31:20.060 --> 00:31:22.185
CARTER MORGAN: I'm going
to play with that question

00:31:22.185 --> 00:31:22.760
a little bit.

00:31:22.760 --> 00:31:24.760
If a data center caught
on fire, would the world

00:31:24.760 --> 00:31:25.480
lose all of its data?

00:31:25.480 --> 00:31:27.438
No, because not all the
data in the whole world

00:31:27.438 --> 00:31:29.018
is stored in one data center.

00:31:29.018 --> 00:31:31.060
STEPHANIE WONG: But what
about aliens or zombies?

00:31:33.770 --> 00:31:34.520
CARTER MORGAN: OK.

00:31:34.520 --> 00:31:35.450
So this is--

00:31:35.450 --> 00:31:37.220
SETH VARGO: What
about zombie aliens?

00:31:37.220 --> 00:31:39.137
CARTER MORGAN: All bets
are off at that point.

00:31:39.137 --> 00:31:41.090
I'm hiding in a data
center if that happens.

00:31:41.090 --> 00:31:45.020
Look, here's the thing we've got
to watch for, or think about.

00:31:45.020 --> 00:31:47.570
When it comes to this,
every organization,

00:31:47.570 --> 00:31:50.000
if you store important
information-- whether it's

00:31:50.000 --> 00:31:51.472
in a file in your
desk somewhere,

00:31:51.472 --> 00:31:53.930
whether it's on a computer,
whether it's in a data center--

00:31:53.930 --> 00:31:57.560
you have to have a plan
for the unfathomable,

00:31:57.560 --> 00:32:00.350
for disasters, for having
to recover your data

00:32:00.350 --> 00:32:01.655
for a national disaster.

00:32:01.655 --> 00:32:03.680
STEPHANIE WONG: OK,
well, what about a spy

00:32:03.680 --> 00:32:05.720
or national disasters
like floods?

00:32:05.720 --> 00:32:08.660
CARTER MORGAN: Yes, you have
to have a plan for as much

00:32:08.660 --> 00:32:09.980
as you possibly can.

00:32:09.980 --> 00:32:11.900
I don't think you can
plan for everything,

00:32:11.900 --> 00:32:13.632
but data recovery is
one of those things,

00:32:13.632 --> 00:32:15.215
and redundancy is
one of those things.

00:32:15.215 --> 00:32:16.470
So you know that there's a--

00:32:16.470 --> 00:32:17.660
SETH VARGO: What about Godzilla?

00:32:17.660 --> 00:32:19.493
CARTER MORGAN: All
right, I'm about to quit.

00:32:19.493 --> 00:32:21.470
[LAUGHTER]

00:32:21.470 --> 00:32:23.690
There's a lot that
could happen, and I'm

00:32:23.690 --> 00:32:25.950
sure everyone here
could go into more.

00:32:25.950 --> 00:32:28.910
But one of the things that
Google does really well

00:32:28.910 --> 00:32:31.850
is they duplicate data
when necessary, they

00:32:31.850 --> 00:32:33.800
have multiple zones,
they have regions.

00:32:33.800 --> 00:32:37.320
So someone like a
customer can say, hey,

00:32:37.320 --> 00:32:41.390
I want to make sure that my
data is replicated across zones

00:32:41.390 --> 00:32:43.520
so that if a zone
goes down I can

00:32:43.520 --> 00:32:45.123
have data still up and online.

00:32:45.123 --> 00:32:46.790
And so that's one of
the things that you

00:32:46.790 --> 00:32:49.910
have to do when you're
securing your own information,

00:32:49.910 --> 00:32:52.730
is set up a disaster
or data recovery plan.

00:32:52.730 --> 00:32:54.320
And Google has some
of this in place,

00:32:54.320 --> 00:32:57.290
too, at the data center
level to help protect you.

00:32:57.290 --> 00:32:59.630
But like Seth and
Stephanie were saying,

00:32:59.630 --> 00:33:01.370
there are so many
different cases.

00:33:01.370 --> 00:33:02.787
Do you guys have
a little bit more

00:33:02.787 --> 00:33:05.390
about how to actually set
this up, or how much of it

00:33:05.390 --> 00:33:07.490
is Google's responsibility
versus how much is

00:33:07.490 --> 00:33:08.990
the customers' responsibility?

00:33:08.990 --> 00:33:09.350
STEPHANIE WONG: Yeah.

00:33:09.350 --> 00:33:11.120
And something that we
already talked about

00:33:11.120 --> 00:33:14.690
is that the SOC, the security
operation center of all Google

00:33:14.690 --> 00:33:17.090
data centers, are also
designed to roll up

00:33:17.090 --> 00:33:19.700
to a regional SOC
for multi-regional

00:33:19.700 --> 00:33:21.480
high-availability purposes.

00:33:21.480 --> 00:33:24.320
So if an on-site SOC
were to be disrupted

00:33:24.320 --> 00:33:26.780
due to an event such
as a natural disaster

00:33:26.780 --> 00:33:29.300
or an emergency
building evacuation,

00:33:29.300 --> 00:33:31.640
then monitoring
instantly rolls over

00:33:31.640 --> 00:33:34.550
to the regional SOC for
continuous, uninterrupted

00:33:34.550 --> 00:33:35.330
monitoring.

00:33:35.330 --> 00:33:40.110
So SOCs act as a center for
emergency and safety response

00:33:40.110 --> 00:33:42.140
in addition to security
responsibilities,

00:33:42.140 --> 00:33:44.630
like Seth mentioned earlier.

00:33:44.630 --> 00:33:46.850
These SOCs monitor
and respond to alarms

00:33:46.850 --> 00:33:49.490
at all of our facilities,
and are constantly

00:33:49.490 --> 00:33:53.270
tracking local and global events
that could impact operations

00:33:53.270 --> 00:33:54.510
at our data centers.

00:33:54.510 --> 00:33:57.380
So really, you can think of
this like high availability

00:33:57.380 --> 00:34:01.100
for physical security, and that
means it's high availability

00:34:01.100 --> 00:34:04.260
for logical security and
data protection as well.

00:34:04.260 --> 00:34:06.620
And then one thing
that is cool to mention

00:34:06.620 --> 00:34:09.020
is that we do run
dozens of drills a year,

00:34:09.020 --> 00:34:11.870
paying unannounced
skilled adversaries to try

00:34:11.870 --> 00:34:14.870
to get past controls
by any means necessary,

00:34:14.870 --> 00:34:16.159
except for violence.

00:34:16.159 --> 00:34:19.580
And this includes
everything from fake badges

00:34:19.580 --> 00:34:23.360
to social engineering, fake
UPS trucks, ladder trucks

00:34:23.360 --> 00:34:27.050
to get over the fence, people
actually hidden in trucks,

00:34:27.050 --> 00:34:30.139
or attempts in different
environments, like thick fog.

00:34:30.139 --> 00:34:32.270
And we usually win.

00:34:32.270 --> 00:34:34.580
After every attempt,
the main thing

00:34:34.580 --> 00:34:36.889
is that we want to run
a blameless post-mortem

00:34:36.889 --> 00:34:39.770
to improve and make the
next attempt even harder.

00:34:39.770 --> 00:34:42.290
And we ensure the strength
of our security controls,

00:34:42.290 --> 00:34:44.610
and then we iterate
controls as needed.

00:34:44.610 --> 00:34:48.382
So this is all about building
a culture of security.

00:34:48.382 --> 00:34:49.090
SETH VARGO: Yeah.

00:34:49.090 --> 00:34:50.750
And we also have an
exercise at Google

00:34:50.750 --> 00:34:53.770
which we call DRT, disaster
and recovery testing, which

00:34:53.770 --> 00:34:56.260
affects both the software
security side and the data

00:34:56.260 --> 00:34:57.760
center security side, where--

00:34:57.760 --> 00:34:59.380
to directly answer
this question,

00:34:59.380 --> 00:35:01.450
what happens if there's
a fire at a data center--

00:35:01.450 --> 00:35:03.790
we have in the past completely
severed a connection

00:35:03.790 --> 00:35:06.490
to a data center to make sure
that our disaster recovery

00:35:06.490 --> 00:35:07.690
protocols were in place.

00:35:07.690 --> 00:35:09.578
Because yeah, you can
write something down.

00:35:09.578 --> 00:35:10.870
You can put it in a Google Doc.

00:35:10.870 --> 00:35:12.620
You can print it out
and put it in a safe.

00:35:12.620 --> 00:35:15.350
But an untested backup
plan is not a plan.

00:35:15.350 --> 00:35:18.220
So we actually write, and we've
written multiple white papers

00:35:18.220 --> 00:35:21.520
about this, where we
actually do some crazy stuff.

00:35:21.520 --> 00:35:23.950
Like we'll cut off the entire
Mountain View office and all

00:35:23.950 --> 00:35:25.510
of our senior
executives' cell phones,

00:35:25.510 --> 00:35:27.218
and make them
inaccessible, and make sure

00:35:27.218 --> 00:35:29.620
the organization can continue
to function from a business

00:35:29.620 --> 00:35:30.640
continuity perspective.

00:35:30.640 --> 00:35:34.300
But we'll also take an entire
data center, quote, "offline,"

00:35:34.300 --> 00:35:36.490
by maybe severing
the fiber connection

00:35:36.490 --> 00:35:39.430
to make sure that our disaster
recovery plans are in place.

00:35:39.430 --> 00:35:40.513
STEPHANIE WONG: All right.

00:35:40.513 --> 00:35:45.250
So our final question of the day
comes from Martin W., who asks,

